An expectation of boons and blessings. Things believed to be true before there is
evidence, written down so that when the evidence arrives it is clear which
beliefs it confirmed and which it demolished.

--

I believe infinite range with distance falloff is a better game than bounded
range. Bounded range makes positioning a question of circles; falloff plus
sightlines makes it a question of ground, and ground is more interesting than
circles.

I believe that units which stop listening once they leave the factory will be
loved rather than resented, because it moves the game from reacting to planning,
and a plan you cannot fiddle with is a plan you have to think about first.

I believe the per-kind timer -- one countdown that heals every tank at once, a
different one that heals every anti-air gun -- will turn out to be the most
important structural decision in the project. Not because of the healing. Because
every periodic effect in the game will be shaped like it, and a game whose every
periodic effect is "compare two integers" is a game that fits on a handheld and
in a box.

I believe deterministic lockstep is the right network model here and would have
been wrong for a game with a hero in it. Commands are rare, pre-planned and
queued; the only thing crossing the radio is intent, and intent is small.

I believe the demo's six unit kinds are enough to find out whether the sightline
idea works, and that the full catalogue should not be started until they have.

I believe the vision's two names for one unit -- the command tank that flies a
plane and the command truck that dies in one hit -- are the same unit, and that
the difference is a slip of the pen rather than a design. This one is a belief
and not a decision; it is written down as an open question.

I believe somebody will draw a pattern that sends every tank into the sea within
the first hour, and that it will be funnier than anything written here.
